Below is another application of decision trees and considerations for using them:
 
 
In rock climbing, Search and Rescue teams can use Decision Trees to rapidly evaluate injury severity, weather, and terrain steepness to standardise emergency evacuation protocols under extreme stress.
 
Some factors to ensure the model performs carefully include: evaluating objective physics (like weight and tension) rather than relying on male-centric historical data that could miscalculate safety margins for shorter or lighter climbers, excluding factors like gear brand to prevent the tree from penalising those using older, inspected equipment and including diverse data from remote crags so the model does not inaccurately assume that cell service or helicopter access are available everywhere.
 
